erclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126923/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126923"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126929/selection/c6db17883a3c5723974a1ae7bcf8aac0dc44319a79f2b4d639798f6bbecb38fa" :answer/id #uuid "fb9d3b5e-b868-5c34-ad6d-4a0d190299b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126929/selection/09bf3654e9f69b5ca610dd173b049f1ae56a4f8d9a43c15e18d5c3947d0ec12e" :answer/id #uuid "5703010e-d3c3-5edc-86ab-f0dfe3ade8f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126929/selection/ed55a7b52d2b7a2f51a42a2b75dc2aa216e43a07251ae04793f3880b315dad4f" :answer/id #uuid "5c5220cb-36ae-5f2a-98fa-4021022baf19" :answer/type :an