930" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126930/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126930"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection/b15855dae622358b9350bebee7a33f3c71d30d094824c7565459870f784686d8" :answer/id #uuid "d99dc2b3-fd5f-52ff-bcde-77e66b2c60fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" :answer/id #uuid "992080da-25af-5cd3-bbab-9c97dcdeeb12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection/50404c7fafbb91d84852a1ba1f9809cd46976e1b687727776c492ab777614df6" :answer/id #uuid "b2fbbe75-a8ee-5bce-ae76-f34295fbf4aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection/2ab879e309744671fc3b29aeb3ee79209cf32a961ed3a99d9bad129c4ac6e7b3" :answer/id #uuid "06a827ef-37c7-576a-bb0a-d00287e60a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection/bea3f221d5f3fc2dce219fdb688470b59cc433144aae8f581c0d206ea46bb378" :answer/id #uuid "a638a634-3b6e-5c16-8e12-49dacba0d732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd7db48-9620-58a5-8ac1-7f1f3a118ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-149683/selection/50404c7fafbb91d84852a1ba1f9809cd46976e1b687727776c492ab777614df6" :answer-field/choices ["q-149683/selection/b15855dae622358b9350bebee7a33f3c71d30d094824c7565459870f784686d8" "q-149683/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" "q-149683/selection/50404c7fafbb91d84852a1ba1f9809cd46976e1b687727776c492ab777614df6" "q-149683/selection/2ab879e309744671fc3b29aeb3ee79209cf32a961ed3a99d9bad129c4ac6e7b3" "q-149683/selection/bea3f221d5f3fc2dce219fdb688470b59cc433144aae8f581c0d206ea46bb378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-149683" :question/id #uuid "bf1b74fd-443c-53f7-bb08-ec2d1aedbadd" :question/math-academy-id "q-149683" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=6,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-149683/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149683"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
